nse  doing anything to persuade the jury it  wasn't him though  nothing no  uh and in a longer form uh  no  uh they just haven't done anything yet  you know we'll see when it gets to the  closing you know this is just one of  those cases that they're gonna have to  put it together in the closing and just  point to the prosecution and say  look at their look at their evidence it  didn't meet the burden of beyond proof  beyond a reasonable doubt  but my god there is a lot of evidence  and there is only one logical suspect so  what they have to do  is overcome logic and have they done  that yet i don't think so every you know  i've been watching along on this trial  uh  listening to an officer today described  that the searches that they did i  thought was really powerful testimony of  going through and digging out the weeds  and sifting through the weeds while  they're searching the ponds they're  laying their case and we say this a lot  brick by brick  but i haven't seen the defense put any  dynamite in that wall just yet  absolutely now terry i have to assume  that when or if chandler gets convicted  these details are what's going to be the  issue when against him having the  harshest punishment right  absolutely and i couldn't agree with  adam moore there's no question that this  evidence is stacked up against this  defendant he is definitely guilty it's  not going to be if it's going to be win  and when they find him guilty it's a  question of how long it's going to take  them but he is going to get the harshest  penalty he is now facing obviously first  degree intentional homicide and in this  state he could serve the rest of his  life in prison so that's what i expect  we'll see how this continues thank you  both and thank you for joining us here  on law and crime daily we'll see you  next time as we discuss justice in  america  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

